scenario_questions.scenario:
A junior has written a quick divider that sometimes crashes or gives "weird" results:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    int a, b;
    printf("Enter a and b: ");
    scanf("%d %d", &a, &b);
    printf("%d\n", a / b);
    return 0;
}
```
Users report crashes on some inputs, and confusion about results like 3/2 printing 1. The team wants a minimum-change fix that keeps it a tiny console tool.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Risks and fixes:
- Unchecked scanf: If input doesn't contain two ints, a or b is uninitialized and used, which is undefined. Fix by checking the return value: ensure scanf("%d %d", &a, &b) == 2, otherwise print an error and exit nonzero.
- Zero divisor: If b == 0, a/b is a runtime error (SIGFPE on many systems). Guard with an explicit check before dividing; report the error and exit nonzero.
- Integer division truncation: If the tool intends mathematical (real) division, a/b truncates. Either document that it's integer division, or cast one operand to double for floating-point division. If floating is chosen, change the format to %f/%.6f as needed.
Concrete minimal patch (integer division retained, with guards):

```c
int a, b;
if (scanf("%d %d", &a, &b) != 2) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Invalid input\n");
    return 1;
}
if (b == 0) {
    fprintf(stderr, "Division by zero\n");
    return 1;
}
printf("%d\n", a / b);
```
Or, if real-number output is required:

```c
if (b == 0) { fprintf(stderr, "Division by zero\n"); return 1; }
printf("%.6f\n", (double)a / (double)b);
```
Rationales: checking scanf prevents use of uninitialized values; guarding b prevents a hardware trap; choosing integer vs floating division aligns behavior with user expectations.

scenario_questions.scenario:
This code tries to count digits on a line until the newline. Sometimes it behaves strangely at end-of-file:

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char c;
    int digits = 0;
    while ((c = getchar()) != '\n' && c != EOF) {
        if (c >= '0' && c <= '9') digits++;
    }
    printf("%d\n", digits);
}
```
On some inputs, at EOF it loops or miscounts.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Root cause: getchar() returns an int in the range of unsigned char or EOF (-1). Storing it in char loses the EOF sentinel, so comparisons like c != EOF are unreliable (EOF may become 255). Also, the loop condition must evaluate the same fetched character against both '\n' and EOF without reading twice.
Corrected loop:

```c
int ch;            // must be int to hold EOF
int digits = 0;
while ((ch = getchar()) != '\n' && ch != EOF) {
    if (ch >= '0' && ch <= '9') digits++;
}
printf("%d\n", digits);
```
This reads once per iteration, stores into an int, and compares to both '\n' and EOF reliably. Short-circuiting ensures that if ch == '\n', the EOF check is not evaluated (but that doesn't matter because ch holds the same value). At true EOF with no newline, the second comparison stops the loop without extra reads.

