scenario_questions.scenario:
You are designing a reusable text utility module. Current code returns a pointer to an internal static buffer, which causes surprising interactions between calls.

```c
// textutil.h
char *join_words(const char *a, const char *b, const char *sep);

// textutil.c
static char buf[256];
char *join_words(const char *a, const char *b, const char *sep) {
    buf[0] = '\0';
    // naive concatenation using strncat-like logic
    // writes into buf and returns buf
    return buf;
}
```
A caller chains two calls:

```c
printf("%s\n", join_words(join_words("alpha","beta","-"), "gamma", ":"));
```
It sometimes prints "alpha-beta:gamma" and sometimes just ":gamma" or worse. You must fix the API and module layout so that:
- No hidden shared buffer state leaks across calls.
- Callers control destination storage.
- Headers are safe to include from multiple .c files.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
A robust design accepts a destination buffer and its size, and returns a status/length so the caller manages memory and detects truncation. Example:

```c
// textutil.h
#ifndef TEXTUTIL_H
#define TEXTUTIL_H
#include <stddef.h>
size_t join_words(char *dst, size_t dstsz,
                  const char *a, const char *b, const char *sep);
#endif
```

Implementation removes any file-scope buffers and only writes to caller-provided memory. It ensures it never writes beyond dstsz and always null-terminates when dstsz>0. It computes lengths using strnlen-like logic rather than sizeof on parameters (because s parameters are pointers after decay). No static locals with module lifetime are used.

A safe call:

```c
char out[64];
size_t n = join_words(out, sizeof out, "alpha", "beta", "-");
// n is the number of characters written (excluding NUL) or truncated length
printf("%s\n", out);
```

Justification: Passing the destination and its capacity lets the function respect the caller's storage and avoids returning pointers to ephemeral or shared buffers. Include guards prevent multiple inclusion. Using size parameters acknowledges that function parameters are pointers (array-to-pointer decay) and sizeof cannot recover the original array length.

scenario_questions.scenario:
A library function titlecase modifies its input string in place.

```c
// title.h
char *titlecase(char *s);

// title.c
#include <ctype.h>
char *titlecase(char *s) {
    int cap = 1;
    for (char *p = s; *p; ++p) {
        if (cap && *p >= 'a' && *p <= 'z') *p = (char)toupper((unsigned char)*p);
        cap = (*p == ' ');
    }
    return s;
}
```
Many callers pass string literals: titlecase("the quick brown fox"); The program sometimes crashes. Your team wants to keep a convenient API but avoid crashes and still allow in-place operation when the buffer is writable.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Fault: Modifying a string literal is undefined behavior. Even though C types string literals as char[], writing to them is not allowed and often segfaults.

One approach is to provide two overload-like functions via different signatures:

```c
// title.h
#ifndef TITLE_H
#define TITLE_H
#include <stddef.h>
// In-place, for writable buffers only
char *titlecase_inplace(char *s);
// Out-of-place: writes into dst and null-terminates when dstsz>0
size_t titlecase_copy(char *dst, size_t dstsz, const char *src);
#endif
```

Implementation of inplace is the current loop but applied to s (assumed writable). Implementation of copy reads from src (const) and writes into dst, never exceeding dstsz-1 and always null-terminating when possible.

Calls:

```c
char buf[] = "the quick brown fox";
puts(titlecase_inplace(buf));  // in-place safe on a writable array

char out[64];
(void)titlecase_copy(out, sizeof out, "the quick brown fox");
puts(out);                    // safe when starting from a literal
```

scenario_questions.scenario:
A shared scratch buffer is declared in a header and used by multiple .c files.

```c
// scratch.h
char scratch[1024];  // intended to be one shared buffer

// a.c
#include "scratch.h"
void fa(void) { scratch[0] = 'A'; }

// b.c
#include "scratch.h"
void fb(void) { scratch[1] = 'B'; }
```
The linker reports: multiple definition of `scratch`. You must fix the design so there is exactly one global buffer visible to both a.c and b.c, declared in the header without causing multiple definitions.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Use extern in the header and a single definition in one .c:

```c
// scratch.h
#ifndef SCRATCH_H
#define SCRATCH_H
extern char scratch[1024];
#endif

// scratch.c (new)
char scratch[1024];
```

Now each including TU sees only a declaration; the single definition in scratch.c provides storage. This resolves multiple definitions and gives a single shared buffer across the program.

Making it static in the header (e.g., static char scratch[1024];) would give each translation unit its own private copy (internal linkage). That can be appropriate for per-file temporaries, but it changes semantics: a.c and b.c would no longer observe each other's writes.

