scenario_questions.scenario:
You maintain a small records library. Each `Employee` owns a heap-allocated `name` and a dynamic list of project codes. Operations include assigning one `Employee` to another, duplicating arrays of `Employee`, and destroying them. Current code:

```c
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>

typedef struct {
    char  *name;     // owned
    int   *projects; // owned, length proj_count
    size_t proj_count;
} Employee;

void employee_assign(Employee *dst, const Employee *src) {
    // current implementation
    *dst = *src; // shallow copy
}

void employee_destroy(Employee *e) {
    free(e->name);
    free(e->projects);
    *e = (Employee){0};
}

Employee *employees_dup(const Employee *a, size_t n) {
    Employee *b = malloc(n * sizeof *b);
    memcpy(b, a, n * sizeof *b); // shallow array copy
    return b;
}
```
After adding these, intermittent double frees and use-after-free appear during reassignment and teardown.

scenario_questions.expected_answer:
Root causes: (1) `employee_assign` performs a shallow copy of owned pointers, causing `dst` and `src` to alias `name` and `projects`. Destroying both double-frees. (2) `employees_dup` `memcpy`s `Employee` structs so all pointer members alias; destroying both arrays double-frees and reading through one after destroying the other is use-after-free. Fixes: define deep-copy semantics and ensure exception safety.

One safe approach:

```c
int employee_copy(Employee *dst, const Employee *src) {
    char  *new_name = NULL;
    int   *new_proj = NULL;

    if (src->name) {
        new_name = strdup(src->name);
        if (!new_name) goto fail;
    }
    if (src->projects && src->proj_count) {
        new_proj = malloc(src->proj_count * sizeof *new_proj);
        if (!new_proj) goto fail;
        memcpy(new_proj, src->projects, src->proj_count * sizeof *new_proj);
    }
    employee_destroy(dst);          // free old ownership
    dst->name = new_name;
    dst->projects = new_proj;
    dst->proj_count = src->proj_count;
    return 0;
fail:
    free(new_name); free(new_proj);
    return -1;
}

void employee_assign(Employee *dst, const Employee *src) {
    (void)employee_copy(dst, src); // handle/propagate error in production
}

Employee *employees_dup(const Employee *a, size_t n) {
    Employee *b = calloc(n, sizeof *b);
    if (!b) return NULL;
    for (size_t i = 0; i < n; i++) {
        if (employee_copy(&b[i], &a[i]) != 0) {
            // roll back
            for (size_t j = 0; j < i; j++) employee_destroy(&b[j]);
            free(b);
            return NULL;
        }
    }
    return b;
}
```
Explanation: (a) Deep-copy each owned pointer and update `proj_count`. (b) Destroy the destination before installing new ownership to avoid leaks. (c) Use temporaries to avoid losing old pointers on allocation failure. (d) In `employees_dup`, perform element-wise deep copies and roll back on failure to avoid leaks and partial aliasing. These changes eliminate pointer aliasing between records, so later `employee_destroy` calls cannot double-free; they also avoid use-after-free because each `Employee` owns independent allocations.

